1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:07,890
Listen.

2
00:00:08,530 --> 00:00:09,050
Practice.

3
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:09,910
Speak.

4
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:12,260
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:14,180 --> 00:00:17,710
Hello everyone and welcome back to our English learning podcast.

6
00:00:18,030 --> 00:00:20,660
Today we have another exciting lesson for you.

7
00:00:20,970 --> 00:00:32,990
Yes, and today's lesson is extremely practical for office workers, business professionals, freelancers, entrepreneurs, and anyone who wants to sound more natural in English conversations at work.

8
00:00:33,590 --> 00:00:37,430
Today's title is Business English Sentences for Fluent Speaking.

9
00:00:38,190 --> 00:00:47,500
In this episode, we'll teach you useful business English sentences, explain their meanings, and show you practical usage through natural office conversations.

10
00:00:47,820 --> 00:00:52,650
The best way to become fluent is to learn sentences you can actually use every day.

11
00:00:52,690 --> 00:00:54,130
Exactly.

12
00:00:54,410 --> 00:01:02,140
Instead of memorizing difficult grammar rules, focus on useful expressions that native speakers use in real business situations.

13
00:01:02,940 --> 00:01:05,650
So, let's begin with our first useful sentence.

14
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:07,530
Let's get started.

15
00:01:08,270 --> 00:01:10,090
This sentence means let's begin.

16
00:01:10,150 --> 00:01:14,740
People often use it at the beginning of meetings, presentations, or discussions.

17
00:01:15,420 --> 00:01:16,340
For example...

18
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:17,790
Good morning, everyone.

19
00:01:18,150 --> 00:01:19,630
Thanks for joining today's meeting.

20
00:01:19,930 --> 00:01:20,650
Let's get started.

21
00:01:21,390 --> 00:01:21,970
Sounds good.

22
00:01:22,470 --> 00:01:24,780
I already prepared the sales report for discussion.

23
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:26,000
Great.

24
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:27,640
Let's review the numbers together.

25
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:30,060
Here's another useful sentence.

26
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:31,640
I'll look into it.

27
00:01:32,430 --> 00:01:36,890
This means I will investigate the problem or I will check the situation.

28
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:39,450
It's a very professional expression.

29
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:41,340
Example conversation.

30
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:45,280
The client said there's an issue with the payment system.

31
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:46,500
I understand.

32
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:48,220
I'll look into it immediately.

33
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:49,370
Thank you.

34
00:01:49,690 --> 00:01:50,850
Please let me know what you find.

35
00:01:51,810 --> 00:01:52,190
Of course.

36
00:01:52,630 --> 00:01:53,990
I'll update you this afternoon.

37
00:01:54,730 --> 00:01:57,130
Here's another common business sentence.

38
00:01:57,150 --> 00:01:58,420
Could you clarify that?

39
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:01,560
Meaning, could you explain that more clearly?

40
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:05,180
This is useful during meetings when something is confusing.

41
00:02:05,980 --> 00:02:07,120
Example conversation.

42
00:02:07,890 --> 00:02:11,150
We need to improve the customer conversion rate next quarter.

43
00:02:11,970 --> 00:02:12,190
Sure.

44
00:02:12,590 --> 00:02:15,470
Could you clarify which area needs the most improvement?

45
00:02:15,930 --> 00:02:18,550
Mainly our online sales funnel.

46
00:02:19,370 --> 00:02:19,750
Got it.

47
00:02:20,070 --> 00:02:20,750
That makes sense.

48
00:02:21,490 --> 00:02:24,930
Another important sentence is, I completely agree.

49
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:27,840
Meaning, I fully support your opinion.

50
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:29,740
Example conversation.

51
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:32,880
I think we should invest more in digital marketing.

52
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:34,820
I completely agree.

53
00:02:35,150 --> 00:02:37,410
Most customers are online now anyway.

54
00:02:38,170 --> 00:02:38,690
Exactly.

55
00:02:39,050 --> 00:02:41,250
Digital campaigns give us better visibility.

56
00:02:41,950 --> 00:02:43,330
Here's another useful one.

57
00:02:43,870 --> 00:02:45,700
That sounds like a great idea.

58
00:02:46,010 --> 00:02:48,280
This means you really like someone's suggestion.

59
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:50,140
Example conversation.

60
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:54,020
What if we organize monthly training sessions for employees?

61
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:56,100
That sounds like a great idea.

62
00:02:56,360 --> 00:02:58,490
It could improve teamwork and communication.

63
00:02:58,540 --> 00:03:01,630
And it would help employees learn new skills, too.

64
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:03,130
Absolutely.

65
00:03:03,970 --> 00:03:06,750
Another important sentence is, I'm working on it.

66
00:03:07,470 --> 00:03:09,680
Meaning, I'm currently doing the task.

67
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:11,700
Example conversation.

68
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:14,400
Have you finished the presentation yet?

69
00:03:14,870 --> 00:03:17,030
Not yet, but I'm working on it right now.

70
00:03:17,770 --> 00:03:18,310
No problem.

71
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:19,980
Just send it to me before lunch.

72
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:21,180
Will do.

73
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:24,000
Here's a very common office sentence.

74
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:25,800
Let's touch base later.

75
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:28,350
Meaning, let's talk again later.

76
00:03:29,050 --> 00:03:32,630
Native speakers use this expression all the time in business environments.

77
00:03:33,390 --> 00:03:34,560
Example conversation.

78
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:37,640
I still need to review the financial report.

79
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:38,900
All right.

80
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:40,780
Let's touch base again this afternoon.

81
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:41,880
Perfect.

82
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:43,850
I should have more information by then.

83
00:03:44,380 --> 00:03:47,890
Another professional sentence is, I appreciate your feedback.

84
00:03:48,610 --> 00:03:51,590
Meaning, thank you for your opinion or suggestions.

85
00:03:52,490 --> 00:03:53,630
Example conversation.

86
00:03:54,370 --> 00:03:58,600
Your presentation was very clear, but maybe the slides could be shorter.

87
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:00,700
I appreciate your feedback.

88
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:03,200
I'll improve the design before tomorrow's meeting.

89
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:04,440
Great.

90
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:06,200
Overall, you did an excellent job.

91
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:07,320
Thank you.

92
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:11,030
Let's continue with another important business expression.

93
00:04:11,490 --> 00:04:13,430
We're running behind schedule.

94
00:04:13,870 --> 00:04:16,870
meaning we're late and not progressing as planned.

95
00:04:17,650 --> 00:04:18,730
Example conversation.

96
00:04:19,490 --> 00:04:21,650
Are we ready for the product launch next week?

97
00:04:22,340 --> 00:04:25,900
Honestly, we're running behind schedule because of technical issues.

98
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:27,440
That's concerning.

99
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:28,990
What's causing the delay?

100
00:04:29,730 --> 00:04:32,370
The testing phase is taking longer than expected.

101
00:04:33,210 --> 00:04:33,670
I see.

102
00:04:33,730 --> 00:04:35,730
Let's discuss possible solutions.

103
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:39,900
Another useful sentence is, could you send me the details?

104
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:42,940
Meaning, please send me more information.

105
00:04:43,510 --> 00:04:44,750
Example conversation.

106
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:47,700
We found a new supplier with lower prices.

107
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:48,840
Really?

108
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:50,900
Could you send me the details later today?

109
00:04:50,940 --> 00:04:52,260
Absolutely.

110
00:04:52,620 --> 00:04:54,190
I'll email everything after the meeting.

111
00:04:54,820 --> 00:04:55,110
Great.

112
00:04:55,330 --> 00:04:56,990
I'd like to compare the costs first.

113
00:04:57,730 --> 00:04:59,490
Here's another excellent sentence.

114
00:04:59,830 --> 00:05:00,730
Let's move forward.

115
00:05:01,410 --> 00:05:03,790
Meaning, let's continue and make progress.

116
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:05,700
Example conversation.

117
00:05:06,420 --> 00:05:08,540
The client finally approved the proposal.

118
00:05:09,380 --> 00:05:10,060
Excellent news.

119
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,640
Let's move forward with production immediately.

120
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:14,730
I'll inform the operations team.

121
00:05:15,430 --> 00:05:15,830
Perfect.

122
00:05:16,550 --> 00:05:19,790
Another sentence you'll hear often is, I'd like your opinion.

123
00:05:20,570 --> 00:05:22,560
Meaning, I want to know what you think.

124
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:24,480
Example conversation.

125
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:28,060
I created two different marketing designs for the campaign.

126
00:05:28,830 --> 00:05:29,390
Nice work.

127
00:05:30,190 --> 00:05:30,470
Thanks.

128
00:05:30,950 --> 00:05:33,310
I'd like your opinion before we make the final decision.

129
00:05:34,070 --> 00:05:36,660
Personally, I prefer the second design.

130
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:37,840
It looks more modern.

131
00:05:38,660 --> 00:05:39,860
I was thinking the same thing.

132
00:05:40,450 --> 00:05:42,450
Here's another useful sentence.

133
00:05:42,470 --> 00:05:43,940
We need to meet the deadline.

134
00:05:44,700 --> 00:05:46,800
Meaning, we must finish on time.

135
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:48,720
Example conversation.

136
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:52,490
The client expects the final report by Friday morning.

137
00:05:53,190 --> 00:05:55,630
Then we need to meet the deadline no matter what.

138
00:05:56,390 --> 00:05:56,710
Agreed.

139
00:05:56,750 --> 00:05:58,900
I'll stay late tonight if necessary.

140
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:01,040
I appreciate your dedication.

141
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:05,610
Another important expression is, I'm afraid there's a problem.

142
00:06:06,510 --> 00:06:08,970
Meaning, unfortunately, there's an issue.

143
00:06:09,540 --> 00:06:10,780
Example conversation.

144
00:06:11,610 --> 00:06:13,190
Did the shipment arrive this morning?

145
00:06:13,910 --> 00:06:15,190
I'm afraid there's a problem.

146
00:06:15,230 --> 00:06:17,850
The delivery was delayed because of bad weather.

147
00:06:17,890 --> 00:06:19,610
That's unfortunate.

148
00:06:20,050 --> 00:06:22,030
Did the supplier give us a new delivery date?

149
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:23,070
Yes.

150
00:06:23,310 --> 00:06:25,180
They expect it tomorrow afternoon.

151
00:06:26,040 --> 00:06:26,380
All right.

152
00:06:26,740 --> 00:06:28,220
Please keep the client informed.

153
00:06:28,960 --> 00:06:29,360
Of course.

154
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:32,120
Here's another useful business sentence.

155
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:34,020
Let's brainstorm some ideas.

156
00:06:34,790 --> 00:06:37,250
Meaning, let's think creatively together.

157
00:06:37,290 --> 00:06:39,230
Example conversation.

158
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:42,500
Sales have been lower this month.

159
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:43,460
I noticed that too.

160
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:46,470
Let's brainstorm some ideas to attract more customers.

161
00:06:47,270 --> 00:06:49,630
Maybe we could launch a social media campaign.

162
00:06:50,450 --> 00:06:51,470
That's a strong idea.

163
00:06:51,510 --> 00:06:54,700
We could also offer limited time discounts.

164
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:56,460
Excellent suggestion.

165
00:06:57,200 --> 00:07:00,350
Another common sentence is, I'll keep you updated.

166
00:07:01,150 --> 00:07:03,870
Meaning, I'll continue giving you new information.

167
00:07:04,610 --> 00:07:05,750
Example conversation.

168
00:07:06,510 --> 00:07:08,140
Have the clients responded yet?

169
00:07:08,610 --> 00:07:11,750
Not yet, but I'll keep you updated as soon as I hear anything.

170
00:07:12,450 --> 00:07:12,830
Perfect.

171
00:07:13,070 --> 00:07:14,350
Thanks for staying on top of it.

172
00:07:15,050 --> 00:07:15,590
No problem.

173
00:07:16,230 --> 00:07:18,220
Here's another fluent business sentence.

174
00:07:18,580 --> 00:07:19,520
Let's stay focused.

175
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:23,020
Meaning, let's continue concentrating on the goal.

176
00:07:23,860 --> 00:07:24,940
Example conversation.

177
00:07:25,690 --> 00:07:28,830
We still have several tasks left before the presentation.

178
00:07:29,690 --> 00:07:30,010
I know.

179
00:07:30,410 --> 00:07:33,290
Let's stay focused and finish everything before 5 p.m.

180
00:07:34,110 --> 00:07:34,450
Agreed.

181
00:07:34,630 --> 00:07:35,920
We're almost done anyway.

182
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:37,500
Exactly.

183
00:07:37,850 --> 00:07:41,880
Another useful expression is, thanks for bringing this to my attention.

184
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:45,240
Meaning, thank you for informing me about this issue.

185
00:07:45,980 --> 00:07:47,120
Example conversation.

186
00:07:47,820 --> 00:07:50,580
There seems to be an error in the monthly budget report.

187
00:07:51,350 --> 00:07:51,650
Really?

188
00:07:51,690 --> 00:07:53,530
Thanks for bringing this to my attention.

189
00:07:53,850 --> 00:07:55,450
I'll review the numbers immediately.

190
00:07:56,310 --> 00:07:56,850
No problem.

191
00:07:57,250 --> 00:07:59,040
I just wanted to make sure we caught it early.

192
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:00,260
Good catch.

193
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,020
Here's one more excellent sentence.

194
00:08:03,040 --> 00:08:04,940
Let's follow up next week.

195
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:07,700
Meaning, let's discuss this again next week.

196
00:08:08,090 --> 00:08:09,310
Example conversation.

197
00:08:10,050 --> 00:08:12,470
We still need final approval from management.

198
00:08:13,300 --> 00:08:13,800
Understood.

199
00:08:14,200 --> 00:08:16,300
Let's follow up next week after the board meeting.

200
00:08:17,020 --> 00:08:17,660
Sounds good to me.

201
00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:18,660
Perfect.

202
00:08:19,460 --> 00:08:26,530
As you can hear, these business English sentences are simple, natural, and extremely useful in real office situations.

203
00:08:27,350 --> 00:08:31,250
The more you practice these expressions, the more fluent and confident you'll become.

204
00:08:31,990 --> 00:08:38,080
Try using these sentences during meetings, phone calls, emails, and daily workplace conversations.

205
00:08:38,550 --> 00:08:41,710
You don't need complicated vocabulary to sound professional.

206
00:08:42,440 --> 00:08:42,920
Exactly.

207
00:08:43,200 --> 00:08:47,080
Simple and clear communication is the key to fluent business English.

208
00:08:47,830 --> 00:08:48,890
Thanks for listening today.

209
00:08:49,590 --> 00:08:52,810
We hope this lesson helps you speak English more naturally at work.

210
00:08:53,610 --> 00:08:54,840
Keep practicing every day.

211
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:59,620
And remember, fluency comes from repetition and real conversation.

212
00:09:00,410 --> 00:09:01,570
See you in the next episode.

213
00:09:02,250 --> 00:09:02,970
Bye, everyone.

214
00:09:04,130 --> 00:09:04,960
Hello everyone.

215
00:09:04,980 --> 00:09:07,810
Welcome back to our English learning podcast.

216
00:09:08,030 --> 00:09:10,850
We're so happy you're practicing English with us today.

217
00:09:10,950 --> 00:09:12,230
Thanks for joining us again.

218
00:09:12,250 --> 00:09:20,160
Today, we have another exciting and practical lesson for anyone who wants to speak fluent business English naturally and confidently.

219
00:09:20,640 --> 00:09:21,180
That's right.

220
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:25,830
Today's topic is business English sentences for fluent speaking.

221
00:09:26,270 --> 00:09:34,150
In this episode, you'll learn useful business English sentences, their meanings, and how people use them in real office conversations.

222
00:09:34,370 --> 00:09:41,110
Instead of difficult grammar explanations, we'll focus on practical communication you can use every day at work.

223
00:09:41,570 --> 00:09:49,390
These are the kinds of sentences professionals use in meetings, presentations, teamwork, emails, and office discussions.

224
00:09:49,370 --> 00:09:53,600
So sit back, relax, and let's improve your business English together.

225
00:09:54,120 --> 00:09:54,980
All right, everyone.

226
00:09:55,360 --> 00:09:56,000
Let's begin.

227
00:09:56,060 --> 00:09:58,500
Here's our first useful sentence.

228
00:09:59,020 --> 00:10:00,470
Let's go over the details.

229
00:10:01,070 --> 00:10:04,550
Go over means to review or discuss something carefully.

230
00:10:05,090 --> 00:10:06,230
Example conversation.

231
00:10:06,610 --> 00:10:11,400
Before we send the proposal to the client, let's go over the details one more time.

232
00:10:11,900 --> 00:10:12,480
Good idea.

233
00:10:12,840 --> 00:10:14,820
I want to make sure everything is accurate.

234
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:15,900
Perfect.

235
00:10:15,880 --> 00:10:18,870
This sentence is extremely common in business meetings.

236
00:10:19,410 --> 00:10:20,050
Absolutely.

237
00:10:20,470 --> 00:10:22,040
Here's another useful sentence.

238
00:10:22,680 --> 00:10:23,460
I'll handle it.

239
00:10:23,960 --> 00:10:29,210
This means I'll take responsibility for it or I'll manage the situation.

240
00:10:29,730 --> 00:10:30,840
Example conversation.

241
00:10:31,300 --> 00:10:34,900
Who's going to contact the supplier about the delayed shipment?

242
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:35,600
Don't worry.

243
00:10:35,900 --> 00:10:37,290
I'll handle it this afternoon.

244
00:10:37,870 --> 00:10:38,130
Great.

245
00:10:38,190 --> 00:10:38,550
Thank you.

246
00:10:39,010 --> 00:10:40,770
Short, simple, and professional.

247
00:10:41,240 --> 00:10:41,760
Exactly.

248
00:10:42,260 --> 00:10:42,900
Next sentence.

249
00:10:43,340 --> 00:10:45,060
We need to stay focused.

250
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:49,970
This means everyone should continue concentrating on the task or goal.

251
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:51,900
Example conversation.

252
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:55,160
We still have several problems to solve before the deadline.

253
00:10:55,700 --> 00:10:58,870
I know, but we need to stay focused and work together.

254
00:10:59,350 --> 00:10:59,710
Agreed.

255
00:11:00,170 --> 00:11:02,550
Great sentence for teamwork situations.

256
00:11:03,050 --> 00:11:03,610
Definitely.

257
00:11:04,030 --> 00:11:06,240
Here's another practical business sentence.

258
00:11:06,820 --> 00:11:08,020
I'll follow up on that.

259
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:13,070
Follow up means checking progress or continuing communication about something.

260
00:11:13,550 --> 00:11:14,690
Example conversation.

261
00:11:15,150 --> 00:11:17,750
Did the client respond to our proposal yet?

262
00:11:17,730 --> 00:11:20,580
Not yet, but I'll follow up on that tomorrow morning.

263
00:11:21,080 --> 00:11:21,620
Sounds good.

264
00:11:21,680 --> 00:11:23,640
Very professional expression.

265
00:11:24,080 --> 00:11:25,800
And extremely common in offices.

266
00:11:26,280 --> 00:11:27,700
Here's another useful phrase.

267
00:11:28,180 --> 00:11:29,020
That makes sense.

268
00:11:29,530 --> 00:11:33,110
This means you understand and agree with an idea or explanation.

269
00:11:33,590 --> 00:11:34,690
Example conversation.

270
00:11:35,150 --> 00:11:39,420
I think we should reduce unnecessary meetings to improve productivity.

271
00:11:39,400 --> 00:11:40,220
That makes sense.

272
00:11:40,580 --> 00:11:43,890
Employees would have more time to focus on important tasks.

273
00:11:44,410 --> 00:11:44,950
Exactly.

274
00:11:45,470 --> 00:11:47,380
Simple but very natural English.

275
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:48,520
Absolutely.

276
00:11:48,980 --> 00:11:50,260
Next sentence.

277
00:11:50,700 --> 00:11:55,970
This means maintaining order and structure during work.

278
00:11:55,990 --> 00:11:57,360
Example conversation.

279
00:11:57,340 --> 00:11:59,580
We have many documents for this project.

280
00:12:00,020 --> 00:12:03,760
Then let's keep things organized so nobody gets confused later.

281
00:12:04,210 --> 00:12:04,790
Good idea.

282
00:12:05,310 --> 00:12:08,490
Organization is very important in business communication.

283
00:12:08,930 --> 00:12:09,470
Definitely.

284
00:12:09,930 --> 00:12:11,670
Here's another excellent sentence.

285
00:12:12,240 --> 00:12:14,300
I'm available any time after lunch.

286
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:18,500
This means you're free for meetings or discussions later in the day.

287
00:12:19,020 --> 00:12:20,100
Example conversation.

288
00:12:20,540 --> 00:12:23,110
When can we discuss the marketing strategy?

289
00:12:23,090 --> 00:12:26,090
I'm available any time after lunch if that works for you.

290
00:12:26,580 --> 00:12:26,980
Perfect.

291
00:12:27,380 --> 00:12:28,780
Let's meet at 2 p.m. then.

292
00:12:29,280 --> 00:12:31,000
Very practical office English.

293
00:12:31,470 --> 00:12:33,610
Yes, especially when scheduling meetings.

294
00:12:34,150 --> 00:12:34,730
Next phrase.

295
00:12:35,210 --> 00:12:37,080
We're getting closer to the deadline.

296
00:12:37,560 --> 00:12:41,100
This means time is running out before the work must be completed.

297
00:12:41,620 --> 00:12:43,090
Example conversation.

298
00:12:43,070 --> 00:12:45,470
Should we continue revising the presentation?

299
00:12:46,070 --> 00:12:47,550
Agreed.

300
00:12:48,220 --> 00:12:54,730
Exactly.

301
00:12:55,610 --> 00:13:02,320
This means you're offering support or assistance.

302
00:13:02,840 --> 00:13:04,280
Example conversation.

303
00:13:04,260 --> 00:13:07,150
Thanks for helping me prepare for tomorrow's presentation.

304
00:13:07,170 --> 00:13:08,170
No problem.

305
00:13:08,530 --> 00:13:10,390
Let me know if you need anything else tonight.

306
00:13:10,910 --> 00:13:11,720
I appreciate it.

307
00:13:12,240 --> 00:13:13,800
Very polite and supportive.

308
00:13:14,320 --> 00:13:15,980
Great for teamwork and leadership.

309
00:13:16,420 --> 00:13:17,040
Next sentence.

310
00:13:17,730 --> 00:13:18,950
I'd like to hear your thoughts.

311
00:13:19,490 --> 00:13:22,230
This means you want someone's opinion or ideas.

312
00:13:23,310 --> 00:13:27,620
I created two possible designs for the advertisement.

313
00:13:27,900 --> 00:13:28,510
Interesting.

314
00:13:29,010 --> 00:13:31,910
I'd like to hear your thoughts before we make the final decision.

315
00:13:32,390 --> 00:13:32,630
Sure.

316
00:13:33,050 --> 00:13:35,210
Personally, I prefer the second design.

317
00:13:35,790 --> 00:13:37,320
Excellent business communication.

318
00:13:37,760 --> 00:13:38,320
Definitely.

319
00:13:38,740 --> 00:13:40,320
Here's another important sentence.

320
00:13:40,780 --> 00:13:41,960
We should plan ahead.

321
00:13:41,980 --> 00:13:46,350
This means preparing early for future situations or problems.

322
00:13:46,890 --> 00:13:47,970
Example conversation.

323
00:13:48,450 --> 00:13:52,920
The holiday season will probably increase customer orders significantly.

324
00:13:52,900 --> 00:14:18,430
then we should plan ahead and hire temporary staff early good thinking planning ahead is very important in business absolutely next phrase i'm confident we can do it this means you believe the team will succeed example conversation the project looks difficult honestly it is challenging but i'm confident we can do it together that's motivating to hear

325
00:14:18,410 --> 00:14:19,790
Great leadership phrase.

326
00:14:22,560 --> 00:14:24,200
Here's another practical sentence.

327
00:14:25,540 --> 00:14:29,890
This means trying to not waste time.

328
00:14:30,470 --> 00:14:35,020
Should we wait until next week to contact the supplier?

329
00:14:38,540 --> 00:14:40,830
Good point.

330
00:14:41,370 --> 00:14:43,150
Very professional expression.

331
00:14:43,220 --> 00:14:45,520
Yes, especially during busy projects.

332
00:14:46,060 --> 00:14:46,660
Next sentence.

333
00:14:47,330 --> 00:14:48,850
I appreciate your patience.

334
00:14:49,390 --> 00:14:53,740
This means thanking someone for waiting calmly during a delay or problem.

335
00:14:54,240 --> 00:14:55,340
Example conversation.

336
00:14:55,860 --> 00:14:56,890
Sorry for the delay.

337
00:14:57,230 --> 00:14:59,630
The system crashed unexpectedly this morning.

338
00:15:00,090 --> 00:15:00,610
No worries.

339
00:15:01,100 --> 00:15:01,390
Thanks.

340
00:15:01,560 --> 00:15:03,640
I really appreciate your patience.

341
00:15:03,620 --> 00:15:07,090
Excellent phrase for customer service in office communication.

342
00:15:07,550 --> 00:15:08,110
Definitely.

343
00:15:08,550 --> 00:15:10,130
Here's another useful expression.

344
00:15:10,670 --> 00:15:12,400
We're moving in the right direction.

345
00:15:12,920 --> 00:15:16,000
This means progress is positive and improving.

346
00:15:16,510 --> 00:15:17,610
Example conversation.

347
00:15:18,050 --> 00:15:20,730
Customer complaints have decreased this month.

348
00:15:20,710 --> 00:15:21,620
That's good news.

349
00:15:21,980 --> 00:15:23,960
It means we're moving in the right direction.

350
00:15:24,460 --> 00:15:25,020
Exactly.

351
00:15:25,460 --> 00:15:26,990
Great motivational sentence.

352
00:15:27,510 --> 00:15:29,550
Very natural business English, too.

353
00:15:30,050 --> 00:15:30,700
Next phrase.

354
00:15:31,160 --> 00:15:32,620
Let's come up with a solution.

355
00:15:33,080 --> 00:15:36,750
Come up with means creating or thinking of an idea.

356
00:15:36,730 --> 00:15:37,950
Example conversation.

357
00:15:38,430 --> 00:15:41,350
The client isn't satisfied with the current proposal.

358
00:15:41,850 --> 00:15:45,380
All right, let's come up with a solution before tomorrow's meeting.

359
00:15:45,860 --> 00:15:46,380
Sounds good.

360
00:15:46,860 --> 00:15:50,080
Problem solving is a huge part of business communication.

361
00:15:50,560 --> 00:15:51,160
Absolutely.

362
00:15:51,580 --> 00:15:53,680
Here's another useful sentence.

363
00:15:53,700 --> 00:15:55,260
I'll make sure it gets done.

364
00:15:55,790 --> 00:15:58,930
This means you promise the task will be completed.

365
00:15:59,430 --> 00:16:00,530
Example conversation.

366
00:16:00,970 --> 00:16:03,610
Can you finish the report by Friday morning?

367
00:16:03,590 --> 00:16:06,240
Yes, I'll make sure it gets done on time.

368
00:16:06,640 --> 00:16:07,100
Perfect.

369
00:16:07,500 --> 00:16:09,360
That sounds confident and reliable.

370
00:16:09,840 --> 00:16:12,530
Exactly the kind of language professionals use.

371
00:16:13,030 --> 00:16:13,630
Next phrase.

372
00:16:14,130 --> 00:16:16,230
We may need to adjust our strategy.

373
00:16:16,710 --> 00:16:20,540
This means changing plans because the situation changed.

374
00:16:20,520 --> 00:16:21,840
Example conversation.

375
00:16:22,400 --> 00:16:24,970
Sales numbers are lower than expected this month.

376
00:16:25,610 --> 00:16:26,110
I see.

377
00:16:26,170 --> 00:16:30,190
We may need to adjust our strategy and improve our marketing efforts.

378
00:16:30,750 --> 00:16:31,710
I agree completely.

379
00:16:32,240 --> 00:16:34,220
Very common in business discussions.

380
00:16:34,700 --> 00:16:37,640
Especially during meetings about performance or planning.

381
00:16:37,680 --> 00:16:40,350
Here's another excellent sentence.

382
00:16:40,370 --> 00:16:42,330
I'll send you the details shortly.

383
00:16:42,850 --> 00:16:44,630
Shortly means very soon.

384
00:16:45,190 --> 00:16:46,270
Example conversation.

385
00:16:46,710 --> 00:16:49,120
Could you share the meeting schedule with me later?

386
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:50,240
Of course.

387
00:16:50,220 --> 00:16:51,900
I'll send you the details shortly.

388
00:16:52,320 --> 00:16:52,760
Thank you.

389
00:16:53,180 --> 00:16:54,510
Professional and polite.

390
00:16:55,110 --> 00:16:57,810
Very useful for emails and office communication.

391
00:16:58,320 --> 00:16:58,940
Next sentence.

392
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:01,500
That's beyond my responsibility.

393
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:05,790
This means something is outside your job duties or authority.

394
00:17:06,290 --> 00:17:07,650
Example conversation.

395
00:17:07,630 --> 00:17:10,100
Can you approve the final company budget?

396
00:17:10,680 --> 00:17:13,120
Actually, that's beyond my responsibility.

397
00:17:13,520 --> 00:17:15,110
You'll need the director's approval.

398
00:17:15,570 --> 00:17:16,050
Understood.

399
00:17:16,770 --> 00:17:19,930
Definitely.

400
00:17:20,450 --> 00:17:28,850
Efficiently means working well without wasting time or energy.

401
00:17:29,350 --> 00:17:30,450
Example conversation.

402
00:17:30,790 --> 00:17:33,130
The team keeps missing deadlines lately.

403
00:17:33,590 --> 00:17:37,400
Then we need to work more efficiently and improve communication.

404
00:17:37,880 --> 00:17:38,300
Agreed.

405
00:17:38,780 --> 00:17:40,670
Very realistic office English.

406
00:17:41,170 --> 00:17:41,770
Absolutely.

407
00:17:42,230 --> 00:17:42,850
Next sentence.

408
00:17:43,550 --> 00:17:45,680
I'd recommend waiting a little longer.

409
00:17:46,160 --> 00:17:49,930
This means giving advice to delay action temporarily.

410
00:17:49,910 --> 00:17:51,350
Example conversation.

411
00:17:51,850 --> 00:17:53,950
Should we launch the product immediately?

412
00:17:53,970 --> 00:17:59,000
Honestly, I'd recommend waiting a little longer until testing is complete.

413
00:17:59,460 --> 00:18:00,360
That sounds safer.

414
00:18:00,920 --> 00:18:02,860
Excellent professional advice sentence.

415
00:18:03,420 --> 00:18:05,530
Very natural in meetings and discussions.

416
00:18:06,010 --> 00:18:08,010
Here's another useful phrase.

417
00:18:08,030 --> 00:18:09,510
Thanks for your cooperation.

418
00:18:09,970 --> 00:18:13,340
This means thanking people for working together respectfully.

419
00:18:13,780 --> 00:18:14,860
Example conversation.

420
00:18:15,320 --> 00:18:19,100
We successfully completed the office relocation project today.

421
00:18:19,080 --> 00:18:20,440
Excellent work, everyone.

422
00:18:20,790 --> 00:18:23,290
Thanks for your cooperation throughout the process.

423
00:18:23,710 --> 00:18:25,830
Teamwork made everything much easier.

424
00:18:26,370 --> 00:18:26,990
Absolutely.

425
00:18:27,480 --> 00:18:31,640
Wow, we practiced so many powerful business English sentences today.

426
00:18:31,700 --> 00:18:32,840
We really did.

427
00:18:32,900 --> 00:18:38,330
And these are all practical expressions professionals use in real workplaces every day.

428
00:18:38,390 --> 00:18:41,600
The more you repeat and practice these sentences,

429
00:18:41,580 --> 00:18:44,520
the more fluent and confident your English will become.

430
00:18:44,560 --> 00:18:50,570
Try using these expressions in your meetings, emails, or even daily conversations with coworkers.

431
00:18:51,090 --> 00:18:52,910
Don't be afraid to practice out loud.

432
00:18:53,350 --> 00:18:56,840
Speaking regularly is the fastest way to improve fluency.

433
00:18:57,320 --> 00:19:01,260
Remember, fluent English is not about using complicated words.

434
00:19:01,760 --> 00:19:02,340
Exactly.

435
00:19:02,720 --> 00:19:06,290
It's about communicating clearly, naturally, and confidently.

436
00:19:06,330 --> 00:19:09,710
Thank you so much for learning English with us today.

437
00:19:09,690 --> 00:19:13,460
We hope this lesson helps you sound more professional and fluent at work.

438
00:19:13,940 --> 00:19:18,610
Keep practicing every day and continue building your confidence step by step.

439
00:19:19,010 --> 00:19:20,570
Thanks again for listening, everyone.

440
00:19:20,990 --> 00:19:24,690
See you next time for another English Learning Podcast conversation.

441
00:19:25,260 --> 00:19:29,260
Hello, everyone, and welcome back to our Business English Speaking Podcast.

442
00:19:29,680 --> 00:19:30,140
I'm Ryan.

443
00:19:30,600 --> 00:19:31,280
And I'm Chloe.

444
00:19:31,500 --> 00:19:33,390
Thank you so much for joining us today.

445
00:19:33,740 --> 00:19:40,090
If you want to improve your English fluency for work and professional communication, today's lesson will really help you.

446
00:19:40,770 --> 00:19:41,630
Absolutely.

447
00:19:41,670 --> 00:19:49,460
Today, we're practicing business English sentences for fluent speaking with real meanings and practical office conversations.

448
00:19:50,100 --> 00:19:59,490
We're going to focus on useful sentences professionals use every day in meetings, offices, presentations, phone calls, and teamwork situations.

449
00:19:59,810 --> 00:20:08,220
And the best part is that we'll explain everything naturally through conversation, so you can learn English the same way native speakers use it at work.

450
00:20:08,820 --> 00:20:11,460
All right, Chloe, let's jump into today's lesson.

451
00:20:11,940 --> 00:20:12,480
Let's do it.

452
00:20:13,140 --> 00:20:16,150
Okay, everyone, here's the first business sentence for today.

453
00:20:16,730 --> 00:20:18,130
Can we go over this together?

454
00:20:18,170 --> 00:20:22,230
This means, can we review or check this together?

455
00:20:23,030 --> 00:20:29,040
Very common in offices when people need help understanding documents, reports, or project plans.

456
00:20:29,480 --> 00:20:30,720
Example conversation.

457
00:20:31,380 --> 00:20:34,600
Ryan, can we go over this sales report together before the meeting?

458
00:20:35,220 --> 00:20:35,610
Of course.

459
00:20:36,050 --> 00:20:37,450
Let's review the numbers carefully.

460
00:20:38,170 --> 00:20:40,430
Simple, practical, and professional.

461
00:20:40,990 --> 00:20:42,380
Here's another excellent sentence.

462
00:20:43,120 --> 00:20:44,180
I'm just following up.

463
00:20:44,780 --> 00:20:49,090
This means you're checking on something again after already discussing it earlier.

464
00:20:49,650 --> 00:20:54,660
Business professionals use this expression constantly in emails and conversations.

465
00:20:54,860 --> 00:20:56,020
Example conversation.

466
00:20:56,700 --> 00:20:57,160
Hi, Claudia.

467
00:20:57,380 --> 00:20:59,880
I'm just following up on the proposal I sent last week.

468
00:21:00,480 --> 00:21:01,440
Thanks for reminding me.

469
00:21:01,700 --> 00:21:02,870
I'll review it this afternoon.

470
00:21:03,510 --> 00:21:05,190
Very natural office English.

471
00:21:05,890 --> 00:21:06,230
All right.

472
00:21:06,510 --> 00:21:07,110
Next sentence.

473
00:21:07,810 --> 00:21:09,450
We should keep everyone informed.

474
00:21:10,120 --> 00:21:14,020
The meaning is everyone should receive updates and information.

475
00:21:14,620 --> 00:21:18,080
Communication is extremely important in business environments.

476
00:21:18,810 --> 00:21:19,810
Example conversation.

477
00:21:20,490 --> 00:21:22,770
The project timeline changed this morning.

478
00:21:23,240 --> 00:21:25,900
then we should keep everyone informed immediately.

479
00:21:26,510 --> 00:21:27,930
Great teamwork language.

480
00:21:28,530 --> 00:21:30,570
Here's another useful sentence for fluent speaking.

481
00:21:31,210 --> 00:21:32,210
Let's break this down.

482
00:21:32,870 --> 00:21:37,580
This means let's explain or divide something into smaller, easier parts.

483
00:21:38,240 --> 00:21:41,830
Managers often use this expression during complicated discussions.

484
00:21:42,530 --> 00:21:43,550
Example conversation.

485
00:21:44,230 --> 00:21:46,710
The marketing strategy seems complicated.

486
00:21:47,480 --> 00:21:47,980
Don't worry.

487
00:21:48,220 --> 00:21:49,860
Let's break this down step by step.

488
00:21:50,440 --> 00:21:52,400
That sounds calm and professional.

489
00:21:52,790 --> 00:21:53,390
Exactly.

490
00:21:53,950 --> 00:21:56,190
Here's another business sentence you'll hear often.

491
00:21:56,870 --> 00:21:58,190
I'm afraid we can't do that.

492
00:21:58,890 --> 00:22:01,060
This is a polite way to say no.

493
00:22:01,820 --> 00:22:05,120
Fluent professionals avoid sounding too direct or rude.

494
00:22:05,760 --> 00:22:06,860
Example conversation.

495
00:22:07,480 --> 00:22:09,970
Can we finish the entire project by tomorrow?

496
00:22:10,650 --> 00:22:13,470
I'm afraid we can't do that without affecting quality.

497
00:22:14,170 --> 00:22:16,730
Much more professional than simply saying no.

498
00:22:17,400 --> 00:22:17,960
Definitely.

499
00:22:18,620 --> 00:22:19,940
All right, next sentence.

500
00:22:20,620 --> 00:22:22,220
We should think long-term.

501
00:22:22,540 --> 00:22:26,560
meaning we should focus on the future, not only the present moment.

502
00:22:27,160 --> 00:22:30,450
Executives and managers use this expression frequently.

503
00:22:31,130 --> 00:22:32,190
Example conversation.

504
00:22:32,890 --> 00:22:35,190
The cheaper option looks attractive right now.

505
00:22:35,910 --> 00:22:39,410
True, but we need to think long-term before making a decision.

506
00:22:40,120 --> 00:22:42,140
Very fluent business communication.

507
00:22:42,740 --> 00:22:44,920
Here's another powerful sentence.

508
00:22:44,940 --> 00:22:46,420
I'd appreciate your support.

509
00:22:47,120 --> 00:22:49,630
This means I would be thankful for your help.

510
00:22:50,060 --> 00:22:53,130
It sounds polite, respectful, and professional.

511
00:22:53,790 --> 00:22:54,810
Example conversation.

512
00:22:55,490 --> 00:23:00,460
I'll be leading the client presentation tomorrow, and I'd appreciate your support during the discussion.

513
00:23:01,120 --> 00:23:02,360
Great leadership English.

514
00:23:02,980 --> 00:23:04,460
Here's another useful expression.

515
00:23:05,100 --> 00:23:06,550
Let's take a closer look.

516
00:23:07,250 --> 00:23:10,270
This means let's examine something more carefully.

517
00:23:10,930 --> 00:23:11,950
Example conversation.

518
00:23:12,650 --> 00:23:14,340
Sales numbers dropped last month.

519
00:23:15,040 --> 00:23:17,360
All right, let's take a closer look at the data.

520
00:23:18,060 --> 00:23:19,770
Very common in meetings.

521
00:23:20,030 --> 00:23:22,820
Here's another sentence fluent professionals use daily.

522
00:23:23,580 --> 00:23:25,100
I understand your concern.

523
00:23:25,780 --> 00:23:28,080
Meaning, I understand why you're worried.

524
00:23:28,120 --> 00:23:32,790
This sentence is excellent for customer service and leadership situations.

525
00:23:33,510 --> 00:23:34,490
Example conversation.

526
00:23:35,190 --> 00:23:37,510
The client is worried about delivery delays.

527
00:23:38,300 --> 00:23:41,260
I understand your concern and we're working on a solution.

528
00:23:41,880 --> 00:23:44,140
Calm, professional and reassuring.

529
00:23:44,820 --> 00:23:45,300
Exactly.

530
00:23:45,900 --> 00:23:47,450
Here's another important sentence.

531
00:23:48,150 --> 00:23:49,650
We need to be flexible.

532
00:23:50,020 --> 00:23:53,340
This means people should be open to changes and adjustments.

533
00:23:54,080 --> 00:23:55,080
Example conversation.

534
00:23:55,720 --> 00:23:58,230
The client requested major design changes.

535
00:23:58,970 --> 00:24:00,690
I know, but we need to be flexible.

536
00:24:01,290 --> 00:24:02,770
Great workplace attitude.

537
00:24:03,330 --> 00:24:04,980
Here's another natural business sentence.

538
00:24:05,660 --> 00:24:07,240
That's outside my area.

539
00:24:07,880 --> 00:24:10,760
Meaning that responsibility belongs to someone else.

540
00:24:11,420 --> 00:24:12,440
Example conversation.

541
00:24:13,010 --> 00:24:14,690
Can you approve the budget increase?

542
00:24:15,410 --> 00:24:17,570
Actually, that's outside my area.

543
00:24:17,890 --> 00:24:19,330
You'll need to speak with finance.

544
00:24:19,720 --> 00:24:21,560
Very practical office English.

545
00:24:22,220 --> 00:24:24,900
Here's another excellent sentence for fluent speaking.

546
00:24:25,650 --> 00:24:26,550
I'll get back to you.

547
00:24:27,230 --> 00:24:29,410
This means I'll give you an answer later.

548
00:24:30,110 --> 00:24:32,380
Extremely common in business communication.

549
00:24:33,020 --> 00:24:34,060
Example conversation.

550
00:24:34,620 --> 00:24:36,320
Can we change the contract terms?

551
00:24:37,060 --> 00:24:40,370
Let me discuss it with management first and I'll get back to you tomorrow.

552
00:24:40,930 --> 00:24:42,230
Professional and polite.

553
00:24:42,850 --> 00:24:44,070
Here's another useful sentence.

554
00:24:44,710 --> 00:24:46,050
We're making good progress.

555
00:24:46,780 --> 00:24:49,120
Meaning things are developing successfully.

556
00:24:49,540 --> 00:24:50,720
Example conversation.

557
00:24:51,280 --> 00:24:52,960
How's the website redesign going?

558
00:24:53,560 --> 00:24:55,890
We're making good progress and should finish on time.

559
00:24:56,490 --> 00:24:58,750
Positive and confident business language.

560
00:24:59,370 --> 00:25:00,090
Here's another one.

561
00:25:00,730 --> 00:25:01,860
Let's stay focused.

562
00:25:02,480 --> 00:25:06,080
Meaning, let's avoid distractions and continue concentrating.

563
00:25:06,740 --> 00:25:07,740
Example conversation.

564
00:25:08,320 --> 00:25:10,950
We're discussing too many unrelated topics.

565
00:25:11,630 --> 00:25:12,030
You're right.

566
00:25:12,470 --> 00:25:14,410
Let's stay focused on today's objective.

567
00:25:14,730 --> 00:25:16,250
Excellent meeting expression.

568
00:25:16,890 --> 00:25:19,420
Here's another sentence professionals use often.

569
00:25:20,100 --> 00:25:21,520
I'd like to point something out.

570
00:25:22,160 --> 00:25:24,860
Meaning you want to mention an important detail.

571
00:25:25,540 --> 00:25:26,510
Example conversation.

572
00:25:27,160 --> 00:25:32,430
Before we finalize the agreement, I'd like to point something out regarding the payment schedule.

573
00:25:33,110 --> 00:25:34,630
Very professional wording.

574
00:25:35,190 --> 00:25:37,140
Here's another practical business sentence.

575
00:25:37,800 --> 00:25:39,600
We may need to reconsider.

576
00:25:40,260 --> 00:25:43,080
This means we might need to change our decision.

577
00:25:43,350 --> 00:25:44,490
Example conversation.

578
00:25:45,130 --> 00:25:47,520
Customer feedback has been mostly negative.

579
00:25:48,180 --> 00:25:51,520
In that case, we may need to reconsider our strategy.

580
00:25:52,200 --> 00:25:53,700
Strong problem-solving English.

581
00:25:54,300 --> 00:25:55,570
Here's another excellent phrase.

582
00:25:56,270 --> 00:25:57,810
Let's keep moving forward.

583
00:25:58,410 --> 00:26:00,750
Meaning, let's continue making progress.

584
00:26:01,450 --> 00:26:04,880
Leaders use this sentence constantly during difficult projects.

585
00:26:05,540 --> 00:26:06,520
Example conversation.

586
00:26:07,160 --> 00:26:08,780
The first phase was challenging.

587
00:26:09,480 --> 00:26:11,700
I know, but let's keep moving forward.

588
00:26:12,020 --> 00:26:13,580
Motivational and professional.

589
00:26:14,140 --> 00:26:15,970
Here's another common business sentence.

590
00:26:16,650 --> 00:26:18,030
I'd like to hear your thoughts.

591
00:26:18,690 --> 00:26:20,610
Meaning you want someone's opinion.

592
00:26:21,310 --> 00:26:22,330
Example conversation.

593
00:26:22,950 --> 00:26:25,090
We're considering changing our pricing model.

594
00:26:25,730 --> 00:26:26,230
Interesting.

595
00:26:26,630 --> 00:26:29,520
I'd like to hear your thoughts before making the final decision.

596
00:26:30,160 --> 00:26:31,520
Great collaborative language.

597
00:26:32,140 --> 00:26:34,240
Here's another useful sentence.

598
00:26:34,260 --> 00:26:35,640
We're facing some challenges.

599
00:26:36,280 --> 00:26:38,770
Meaning there are difficulties or problems.

600
00:26:39,490 --> 00:26:40,630
Example conversation.

601
00:26:41,060 --> 00:26:43,410
How's the overseas expansion project going?

602
00:26:43,990 --> 00:26:46,610
We're facing some challenges with shipping and logistics.

603
00:26:47,370 --> 00:26:49,250
Very natural workplace English.

604
00:26:49,890 --> 00:26:51,640
Here's another strong business expression.

605
00:26:52,260 --> 00:26:53,400
Let's be realistic.

606
00:26:54,120 --> 00:26:56,900
Meaning, let's think practically and honestly.

607
00:26:57,530 --> 00:26:58,530
Example conversation.

608
00:26:59,110 --> 00:27:01,150
Can we double sales in one month?

609
00:27:01,890 --> 00:27:02,990
Let's be realistic.

610
00:27:03,210 --> 00:27:05,900
That would be extremely difficult.

611
00:27:06,060 --> 00:27:09,180
Here's another practical sentence.

612
00:27:09,520 --> 00:27:11,460
I take full responsibility.

613
00:27:12,020 --> 00:27:14,980
Meaning you accept blame for a mistake or issue.

614
00:27:15,680 --> 00:27:18,120
Strong leaders say this when problems happen.

615
00:27:18,790 --> 00:27:19,830
Example conversation.

616
00:27:20,490 --> 00:27:22,730
The client received the wrong file yesterday.

617
00:27:23,450 --> 00:27:24,110
I understand.

618
00:27:24,130 --> 00:27:26,630
I take full responsibility for the mistake.

619
00:27:27,390 --> 00:27:29,820
Very mature and professional communication.

620
00:27:30,400 --> 00:27:30,960
Definitely.

621
00:27:31,600 --> 00:27:34,100
Here's another fluent business sentence.

622
00:27:34,120 --> 00:27:35,180
Thanks for bringing this up.

623
00:27:35,800 --> 00:27:38,950
Meaning thank you for mentioning the issue or topic.

624
00:27:39,210 --> 00:27:40,360
Example conversation.

625
00:27:41,060 --> 00:27:44,460
I think there may be a problem with the current budget plan.

626
00:27:44,480 --> 00:27:45,540
Thanks for bringing this up.

627
00:27:45,870 --> 00:27:47,270
Let's review the numbers together.

628
00:27:47,910 --> 00:27:49,210
Great teamwork expression.

629
00:27:49,810 --> 00:27:51,370
Here's another important sentence.

630
00:27:52,010 --> 00:27:54,080
We should avoid unnecessary risks.

631
00:27:54,700 --> 00:27:58,380
Meaning, don't do dangerous or careless things in business.

632
00:27:59,080 --> 00:28:00,090
Example conversation.

633
00:28:00,750 --> 00:28:03,330
Should we launch the product without additional testing?

634
00:28:04,070 --> 00:28:06,630
No, we should avoid unnecessary risks.

635
00:28:07,380 --> 00:28:08,920
Smart business thinking.

636
00:28:09,120 --> 00:28:11,420
Here's another excellent sentence for fluent speaking.

637
00:28:12,060 --> 00:28:13,520
Everything is under control.

638
00:28:14,180 --> 00:28:16,900
Meaning the situation is being managed properly.

639
00:28:17,530 --> 00:28:18,530
Example conversation.

640
00:28:19,070 --> 00:28:20,970
The client sounded upset earlier.

641
00:28:21,650 --> 00:28:22,150
Don't worry.

642
00:28:22,390 --> 00:28:23,630
Everything is under control now.

643
00:28:24,230 --> 00:28:26,150
Calm and confident communication.

644
00:28:26,780 --> 00:28:28,300
Here's another useful expression.

645
00:28:29,020 --> 00:28:31,460
I'd appreciate your patience and understanding.

646
00:28:32,160 --> 00:28:36,930
This means you're thankful someone stayed calm and cooperative during a difficult situation.

647
00:28:37,570 --> 00:28:38,670
Example conversation.

648
00:28:39,100 --> 00:28:41,890
The project delivery took longer than expected.

649
00:28:42,550 --> 00:28:46,540
We apologize for the delay and appreciate your patience and understanding.

650
00:28:47,220 --> 00:28:49,320
Very professional customer communication.

651
00:28:49,980 --> 00:28:53,020
Here's another sentence fluent professionals love using.

652
00:28:53,700 --> 00:28:55,270
Let's aim for excellence.

653
00:28:55,950 --> 00:28:59,090
Meaning let's try to achieve the highest quality possible.

654
00:28:59,690 --> 00:29:00,760
Example conversation.

655
00:29:01,340 --> 00:29:03,480
The competition in the market is increasing.

656
00:29:04,160 --> 00:29:06,780
Then let's aim for excellence in everything we do.

657
00:29:07,560 --> 00:29:08,710
Absolutely.

658
00:29:08,990 --> 00:29:12,860
Wow, Chloe, we covered so many practical business sentences today.

659
00:29:13,440 --> 00:29:18,560
And every sentence is useful for real office situations, not just textbook learning.

660
00:29:19,270 --> 00:29:19,810
Exactly.

661
00:29:20,290 --> 00:29:26,320
If learners practice these sentences regularly, they'll sound much more natural and fluent in professional conversations.

662
00:29:26,960 --> 00:29:29,480
The key is repetition and real usage.

663
00:29:30,220 --> 00:29:31,520
Don't just read the sentences.

664
00:29:31,880 --> 00:29:32,830
Speak them out loud.

665
00:29:33,570 --> 00:29:37,930
Use them in your meetings, presentations, emails, and conversations at work.

666
00:29:38,250 --> 00:29:41,680
Little by little, fluent business English will become natural for you.

667
00:29:42,240 --> 00:29:44,400
Thanks so much for learning with us today, everyone.

668
00:29:44,980 --> 00:29:49,330
We hope this podcast helps you speak English more confidently in professional situations.

669
00:29:49,930 --> 00:29:51,430
Keep practicing every day.

670
00:29:51,470 --> 00:29:55,720
And we'll see you again soon with another business English conversation lesson.

671
00:29:56,280 --> 00:29:57,020
Take care, everyone.

672
00:29:57,600 --> 00:29:58,140
Bye, everyone.

673
00:29:58,570 --> 00:30:02,350
Hello, everyone, and welcome back to our English learning podcast.

674
00:30:02,370 --> 00:30:04,170
We're very happy to have you here today.

675
00:30:04,590 --> 00:30:05,730
Thanks for joining us again.

676
00:30:06,190 --> 00:30:11,820
If you want to improve your business English and sound more natural at work, today's lesson is perfect for you.

677
00:30:12,260 --> 00:30:12,720
That's right.

678
00:30:12,840 --> 00:30:17,250
Today's topic is real business English conversations in 80 minutes.

679
00:30:17,950 --> 00:30:22,950
In this episode, you'll hear practical office conversations that happen during a busy work day.

680
00:30:23,390 --> 00:30:32,320
We'll talk about meetings, deadlines, teamwork, presentations, office problems, client communication, and professional small talk.

681
00:30:32,670 --> 00:30:37,320
These are realistic conversations that employees use every day in international companies.

682
00:30:37,880 --> 00:30:41,370
So relax, listen carefully, and practice speaking along with us.

683
00:30:42,070 --> 00:30:42,970
All right, everyone.

684
00:30:43,370 --> 00:30:45,990
Let's begin today's business English conversation.

685
00:30:46,610 --> 00:30:47,590
Good morning, everyone.

686
00:30:48,060 --> 00:30:50,260
Thanks for joining today's strategy meeting.

687
00:30:50,580 --> 00:30:53,400
We have a busy schedule, so let's get started right away.

688
00:30:54,020 --> 00:30:54,500
Sounds good.

689
00:30:55,160 --> 00:30:57,890
Before we begin, does everyone have the meeting agenda?

690
00:30:58,470 --> 00:31:00,130
Yes, I received it this morning.

691
00:31:00,550 --> 00:31:01,070
Perfect.

692
00:31:01,550 --> 00:31:08,180
Today we'll discuss sales performance, the upcoming product launch, customer feedback, and next quarter's goals.

693
00:31:08,780 --> 00:31:10,500
That's a lot to cover in one meeting.

694
00:31:11,140 --> 00:31:15,270
True, but I think we can manage our time well if we stay focused.

695
00:31:15,290 --> 00:31:15,630
Agreed.

696
00:31:16,290 --> 00:31:18,470
All right, let's start with sales performance.

697
00:31:18,950 --> 00:31:20,800
Kevin, could you give us the latest update?

698
00:31:21,420 --> 00:31:21,680
Sure.

699
00:31:21,700 --> 00:31:26,480
Overall, sales increased by 18% this quarter compared to the previous one.

700
00:31:27,030 --> 00:31:28,110
That's excellent news.

701
00:31:28,590 --> 00:31:30,270
Which products performed the best?

702
00:31:30,650 --> 00:31:33,500
Our online subscription service showed the biggest growth.

703
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:36,300
Customer demand has been very strong recently.

704
00:31:36,900 --> 00:31:37,400
Interesting.

705
00:31:37,940 --> 00:31:38,700
Do we know why?

706
00:31:38,720 --> 00:31:43,110
Mainly because of the new advertising campaign and social media promotions.

707
00:31:43,850 --> 00:31:45,210
Great job, marketing team.

708
00:31:45,770 --> 00:31:46,190
Thank you.

709
00:31:46,610 --> 00:31:48,630
Everyone worked really hard on that campaign.

710
00:31:49,260 --> 00:31:51,300
Did we face any challenges during the quarter?

711
00:31:51,940 --> 00:31:52,200
Yes.

712
00:31:52,440 --> 00:31:54,820
Shipping delays caused some customer complaints.

713
00:31:55,500 --> 00:31:56,560
That issue again.

714
00:31:56,580 --> 00:31:59,670
We definitely need to improve our delivery process.

715
00:32:00,040 --> 00:32:00,580
I agree.

716
00:32:00,900 --> 00:32:03,400
Some customers waited much longer than expected.

717
00:32:04,000 --> 00:32:06,400
Have we discussed this with the logistics department yet?

718
00:32:07,070 --> 00:32:09,870
Not officially, but I spoke with their manager yesterday.

719
00:32:09,910 --> 00:32:11,450
And what did he say?

720
00:32:12,070 --> 00:32:15,260
They're currently understaffed and dealing with supply chain problems.

721
00:32:16,060 --> 00:32:16,620
I see.

722
00:32:16,680 --> 00:32:20,040
That explains the delays, but we still need a solution quickly.

723
00:32:20,660 --> 00:32:21,190
Definitely.

724
00:32:21,470 --> 00:32:24,270
Customer satisfaction is starting to drop because of it.

725
00:32:24,950 --> 00:32:25,330
All right.

726
00:32:25,430 --> 00:32:28,240
Let's schedule a separate meeting with logistics this week.

727
00:32:28,640 --> 00:32:29,920
I'll arrange it this afternoon.

728
00:32:30,560 --> 00:32:31,020
Perfect.

729
00:32:31,700 --> 00:32:33,380
Excuse me, may I add something?

730
00:32:33,970 --> 00:32:34,350
Of course.

731
00:32:35,090 --> 00:32:38,790
Maybe we should also improve communication with customers during delays.

732
00:32:39,520 --> 00:32:40,480
What do you mean exactly?

733
00:32:41,060 --> 00:32:44,520
Customers get frustrated when they don't receive updates about their orders.

734
00:32:45,160 --> 00:32:45,850
That's true.

735
00:32:46,590 --> 00:32:49,770
If we send regular updates, customers may feel less annoyed.

736
00:32:50,450 --> 00:32:51,210
Excellent point.

737
00:32:51,610 --> 00:32:54,360
Communication is very important during problems.

738
00:32:54,590 --> 00:32:55,170
Exactly.

739
00:32:55,490 --> 00:32:58,620
People are usually more understanding when they know what's happening.

740
00:32:59,260 --> 00:32:59,740
All right.

741
00:32:59,820 --> 00:33:01,500
Let's make that part of our action plan.

742
00:33:02,100 --> 00:33:02,560
Sounds good.

743
00:33:03,240 --> 00:33:06,740
Moving on to the next topic, let's discuss the new product launch.

744
00:33:07,100 --> 00:33:08,260
Emily, could you update us?

745
00:33:08,980 --> 00:33:09,220
Sure.

746
00:33:09,240 --> 00:33:11,410
The launch campaign is almost ready.

747
00:33:11,750 --> 00:33:14,850
The design team finalized the promotional materials yesterday.

748
00:33:15,550 --> 00:33:16,030
Excellent.

749
00:33:16,510 --> 00:33:17,950
What about the website updates?

750
00:33:18,610 --> 00:33:23,640
The IT department is still testing a few features, but everything should be completed by Friday.

751
00:33:24,040 --> 00:33:24,400
Great.

752
00:33:24,760 --> 00:33:27,060
Are we still launching on schedule next Monday?

753
00:33:27,720 --> 00:33:28,560
At the moment, yes.

754
00:33:29,240 --> 00:33:30,350
Any major concerns?

755
00:33:31,010 --> 00:33:34,030
Honestly, I'm slightly worried about the customer support team.

756
00:33:34,690 --> 00:33:34,990
Why?

757
00:33:35,650 --> 00:33:39,100
We expect a large increase in customer questions after the launch.

758
00:33:39,760 --> 00:33:40,460
Good point.

759
00:33:40,720 --> 00:33:42,320
Do we have enough staff available?

760
00:33:42,920 --> 00:33:44,600
Probably not during peak hours.

761
00:33:45,200 --> 00:33:47,470
Then we should prepare additional support coverage.

762
00:33:48,170 --> 00:33:48,570
I agree.

763
00:33:49,030 --> 00:33:51,770
Maybe temporary staff could help for the first few weeks.

764
00:33:52,410 --> 00:33:53,450
That sounds reasonable.

765
00:33:53,870 --> 00:33:57,000
We should also prepare answers for frequently asked questions.

766
00:33:57,740 --> 00:33:58,500
Excellent idea.

767
00:33:58,520 --> 00:34:00,740
That would help the support team respond faster.

768
00:34:01,400 --> 00:34:01,870
Exactly.

769
00:34:01,880 --> 00:34:04,730
It would also create more consistent communication.

770
00:34:05,410 --> 00:34:05,790
All right.

771
00:34:05,870 --> 00:34:08,720
Let's assign someone to prepare an FAQ document.

772
00:34:09,380 --> 00:34:11,200
I can work on that with the support department.

773
00:34:11,720 --> 00:34:12,080
Perfect.

774
00:34:12,280 --> 00:34:12,640
Thank you.

775
00:34:12,660 --> 00:34:13,740
No problem.

776
00:34:14,460 --> 00:34:17,190
By the way, how are we doing with the launch event itself?

777
00:34:17,850 --> 00:34:19,410
The venue has already been booked.

778
00:34:19,730 --> 00:34:22,070
We're expecting 150 guests.

779
00:34:22,410 --> 00:34:23,910
Wow, that's more than I expected.

780
00:34:24,530 --> 00:34:26,610
Yes, interest has been very strong.

781
00:34:27,220 --> 00:34:28,700
Have we invited the media yet?

782
00:34:29,300 --> 00:34:31,320
Yes, invitations were sent last week.

783
00:34:31,700 --> 00:34:34,030
Several journalists already confirmed attendance.

784
00:34:34,730 --> 00:34:35,210
Excellent.

785
00:34:35,570 --> 00:34:38,370
This launch could really improve our company's visibility.

786
00:34:38,990 --> 00:34:39,690
I think so, too.

787
00:34:40,390 --> 00:34:43,940
All right, everyone, let's take a short five-minute break before continuing.

788
00:34:44,520 --> 00:34:44,960
Good idea.

789
00:34:45,260 --> 00:34:46,520
I definitely need coffee.

790
00:34:47,160 --> 00:34:47,660
Same here.

791
00:34:48,350 --> 00:34:50,830
Hey, how are things going with your department lately?

792
00:34:51,160 --> 00:34:52,700
Honestly pretty busy.

793
00:34:52,950 --> 00:34:55,550
We've been handling several projects at the same time.

794
00:34:56,230 --> 00:34:57,090
I know the feeling.

795
00:34:57,570 --> 00:35:00,510
My inbox has been completely full every day this week.

796
00:35:01,150 --> 00:35:01,730
Same here.

797
00:35:02,190 --> 00:35:04,820
Every time I answer one email, three more arrive.

798
00:35:05,520 --> 00:35:06,220
Exactly.

799
00:35:06,740 --> 00:35:08,820
Sometimes it feels impossible to keep up.

800
00:35:09,420 --> 00:35:10,940
At least the weekend is getting closer.

801
00:35:11,560 --> 00:35:12,120
That's true.

802
00:35:12,700 --> 00:35:13,240
Any plans?

803
00:35:13,920 --> 00:35:16,710
I'm thinking about taking a short trip to the countryside.

804
00:35:17,130 --> 00:35:18,930
I need a break from screens and meetings.

805
00:35:19,570 --> 00:35:20,750
That sounds relaxing.

806
00:35:21,030 --> 00:35:21,590
What about you?

807
00:35:22,230 --> 00:35:24,760
Honestly, I'll probably just stay home and sleep.

808
00:35:25,380 --> 00:35:27,040
Honestly, that sounds amazing, too.

809
00:35:27,720 --> 00:35:29,580
All right, should we head back to the meeting room?

810
00:35:30,180 --> 00:35:30,660
Let's do it.

811
00:35:31,340 --> 00:35:32,990
Okay, everyone, welcome back.

812
00:35:33,010 --> 00:35:35,470
Let's continue with customer feedback analysis.

813
00:35:36,170 --> 00:35:37,990
Emma, could you share the survey results?

814
00:35:38,750 --> 00:35:39,030
Sure.

815
00:35:39,410 --> 00:35:43,740
Overall, satisfaction remains high, but there are a few areas that need improvement.

816
00:35:44,440 --> 00:35:45,700
Which areas specifically?

817
00:35:46,300 --> 00:35:50,030
Customers want faster response times and clearer communication.

818
00:35:50,340 --> 00:35:52,250
That seems to be a common issue lately.

819
00:35:52,850 --> 00:35:56,070
Yes, several customers mentioned delayed email replies.

820
00:35:56,890 --> 00:35:57,560
I understand.

821
00:35:57,600 --> 00:35:59,680
Our teams have been overloaded recently.

822
00:36:00,340 --> 00:36:03,180
True, but customers still expect quick responses.

823
00:36:03,950 --> 00:36:04,550
Absolutely.

824
00:36:04,950 --> 00:36:07,230
We need to balance workload more effectively.

825
00:36:07,910 --> 00:36:11,460
Maybe we should introduce a shared support system between departments.

826
00:36:12,240 --> 00:36:13,200
Interesting idea.

827
00:36:13,500 --> 00:36:14,680
Could you explain further?

828
00:36:15,000 --> 00:36:20,950
For example, during busy periods, employees from other teams could help answer simple customer questions.

829
00:36:21,650 --> 00:36:23,650
That could reduce pressure on the support team.

830
00:36:24,350 --> 00:36:24,890
Exactly.

831
00:36:25,530 --> 00:36:26,450
What does everyone think?

832
00:36:27,070 --> 00:36:30,460
I like the idea, but employees would need proper training first.

833
00:36:31,080 --> 00:36:31,720
Good point.

834
00:36:31,900 --> 00:36:34,540
We can't risk giving customers incorrect information.

835
00:36:35,220 --> 00:36:37,830
Maybe short training sessions would solve that problem.

836
00:36:38,470 --> 00:36:39,530
I think that's manageable.

837
00:36:39,830 --> 00:36:41,910
Let's explore the idea further next week.

838
00:36:42,570 --> 00:36:43,450
Sounds good.

839
00:36:43,550 --> 00:36:46,560
All right, next topic, budget planning for next quarter.

840
00:36:47,160 --> 00:36:49,000
This is always the difficult part.

841
00:36:49,680 --> 00:36:50,460
Very true.

842
00:36:50,780 --> 00:36:54,590
We need to reduce unnecessary spending while still supporting growth.

843
00:36:55,270 --> 00:36:57,490
Do we already know the projected budget limit?

844
00:36:58,110 --> 00:37:01,290
Finance expects a slightly smaller budget than last quarter.

845
00:37:01,890 --> 00:37:02,960
That could be challenging.

846
00:37:03,560 --> 00:37:07,300
Yes, especially because several departments requested additional resources.

847
00:37:07,940 --> 00:37:09,960
Which departments requested increases?

848
00:37:10,420 --> 00:37:13,400
Marketing, IT, and customer support.

849
00:37:14,060 --> 00:37:16,650
Honestly, all three departments have valid reasons.

850
00:37:17,410 --> 00:37:17,890
I agree.

851
00:37:17,930 --> 00:37:24,420
IT needs system upgrades, customer support needs staff, and marketing wants to expand campaigns.

852
00:37:25,180 --> 00:37:25,800
Exactly.

853
00:37:26,200 --> 00:37:28,140
Balancing priorities won't be easy.

854
00:37:28,760 --> 00:37:32,850
Perhaps we should focus on investments that generate the highest long-term value.

855
00:37:33,410 --> 00:37:34,510
That's a smart approach.

856
00:37:35,150 --> 00:37:39,160
For example, improving customer support could increase customer retention.

857
00:37:39,490 --> 00:37:40,170
Very true.

858
00:37:40,570 --> 00:37:42,760
Happy customers are more likely to stay loyal.

859
00:37:43,420 --> 00:37:46,880
And stronger IT systems could improve efficiency company-wide.

860
00:37:47,400 --> 00:37:48,440
Another excellent point.

861
00:37:49,100 --> 00:37:52,380
Maybe marketing can delay some campaigns until later in the year.

862
00:37:53,000 --> 00:37:53,820
That's possible.

863
00:37:54,090 --> 00:37:55,930
We should discuss it with their manager first.

864
00:37:56,590 --> 00:37:57,110
Definitely.

865
00:37:57,810 --> 00:37:58,210
All right.

866
00:37:58,410 --> 00:38:00,550
Let's move on to team performance reviews.

867
00:38:01,310 --> 00:38:01,550
Sure.

868
00:38:01,590 --> 00:38:05,120
Overall, employee productivity has improved significantly.

869
00:38:05,680 --> 00:38:06,600
That's good to hear.

870
00:38:06,950 --> 00:38:10,670
However, several employees reported feeling stressed and overwhelmed.

871
00:38:11,320 --> 00:38:12,260
I'm not surprised.

872
00:38:12,500 --> 00:38:14,180
The workload has been intense lately.

873
00:38:14,840 --> 00:38:15,520
Exactly.

874
00:38:15,980 --> 00:38:18,950
Some people have worked overtime for several weeks in a row.

875
00:38:19,470 --> 00:38:21,190
That's not sustainable long-term.

876
00:38:21,910 --> 00:38:22,990
I completely agree.

877
00:38:23,620 --> 00:38:27,160
Maybe we should encourage employees to take more breaks and vacation time.

878
00:38:27,780 --> 00:38:29,110
That could help morale a lot.

879
00:38:29,610 --> 00:38:33,510
We should also remind managers to monitor team stress levels carefully.

880
00:38:33,930 --> 00:38:34,490
Definitely.

881
00:38:34,830 --> 00:38:37,940
Burnout can seriously damage productivity and motivation.

882
00:38:38,680 --> 00:38:39,320
Absolutely.

883
00:38:39,740 --> 00:38:42,080
Healthy employees perform better in the long run.

884
00:38:42,700 --> 00:38:46,630
By the way, several employees requested more flexible work schedules, too.

885
00:38:47,250 --> 00:38:47,530
Really?

886
00:38:48,150 --> 00:38:48,390
Yes.

887
00:38:48,910 --> 00:38:51,860
Many people want hybrid work options or flexible hours.

888
00:38:52,520 --> 00:38:55,580
But we still need enough office coverage during business hours.

889
00:38:56,280 --> 00:38:56,600
True.

890
00:38:56,950 --> 00:38:58,270
We'd need a clear system.

891
00:38:58,870 --> 00:39:02,770
Let's research possible flexible work policies and discuss them next month.

892
00:39:03,210 --> 00:39:03,990
Sounds like a plan.

893
00:39:04,670 --> 00:39:05,390
All right, everyone.

894
00:39:05,430 --> 00:39:06,730
We're almost out of time.

895
00:39:06,790 --> 00:39:09,000
Any final updates before we end the meeting?

896
00:39:09,660 --> 00:39:10,920
I have one quick reminder.

897
00:39:11,200 --> 00:39:14,600
The investor presentation rehearsal is scheduled for Thursday morning.

898
00:39:15,180 --> 00:39:15,440
Right.

899
00:39:15,760 --> 00:39:16,890
Thanks for reminding everyone.

900
00:39:17,270 --> 00:39:19,530
Please come prepared with your slides finalized.

901
00:39:20,170 --> 00:39:20,730
Absolutely.

902
00:39:21,370 --> 00:39:21,870
Anything else?

903
00:39:22,570 --> 00:39:23,210
Nothing from me.

904
00:39:23,860 --> 00:39:24,390
All right, then.

905
00:39:24,420 --> 00:39:26,620
I think we covered everything on today's agenda.

906
00:39:27,160 --> 00:39:29,620
This was actually a very productive meeting.

907
00:39:29,820 --> 00:39:30,420
I agree.

908
00:39:30,480 --> 00:39:33,140
Thanks, everyone, for your hard work and ideas today.

909
00:39:33,780 --> 00:39:35,170
Have a great afternoon, everyone.

910
00:39:35,770 --> 00:39:36,170
You too.

911
00:39:36,490 --> 00:39:37,050
See you tomorrow.

912
00:39:37,670 --> 00:39:39,950
Wow, that felt like a real office meeting.

913
00:39:40,590 --> 00:39:41,190
Exactly.

914
00:39:41,530 --> 00:39:45,600
These are the kinds of conversations professionals have every single day at work.

915
00:39:46,200 --> 00:39:53,200
We talked about project management, customer service, teamwork, deadlines, budgeting, and employee well-being.

916
00:39:53,800 --> 00:39:57,770
And all of those topics are extremely important in modern business English.

917
00:39:58,140 --> 00:40:02,820
The best way to improve your English is by listening to natural conversations repeatedly.

918
00:40:02,860 --> 00:40:07,250
You can pause the lesson, repeat the phrases, and practice speaking out loud.

919
00:40:07,870 --> 00:40:10,860
Don't worry if you don't understand every word immediately.

920
00:40:11,520 --> 00:40:14,660
Confidence grows step by step with regular practice.

921
00:40:15,320 --> 00:40:16,380
Thanks for listening, everyone.

922
00:40:16,640 --> 00:40:24,090
We'll see you next time with another Real English Conversation podcast.

923
00:40:24,310 --> 00:40:24,990
Hi, everyone.

924
00:40:25,190 --> 00:40:26,270
Thanks for joining us again.

925
00:40:26,790 --> 00:40:30,000
Today we have another practical English training session for you.

926
00:40:30,420 --> 00:40:31,260
I'm Ryan.

927
00:40:31,280 --> 00:40:32,120
And I'm Sophia.

928
00:40:32,620 --> 00:40:38,990
Today's topic is a new one, Business English Sentences for Fluent Speaking, Office Reality Edition.

929
00:40:39,370 --> 00:40:44,940
In this episode, we'll continue with sentence, meaning, usage, and real conversational examples.

930
00:40:45,340 --> 00:40:51,450
But this time, everything will feel like real workplace situations happening throughout a normal office day.

931
00:40:51,600 --> 00:40:56,720
From morning updates to client calls, problem solving, teamwork, and end-of-day reporting.

932
00:40:57,060 --> 00:41:00,890
So listen carefully and imagine yourself inside these conversations.

933
00:41:01,330 --> 00:41:01,810
Let's begin.

934
00:41:01,870 --> 00:41:03,170
First sentence.

935
00:41:03,770 --> 00:41:06,760
Let's align our priorities before we proceed.

936
00:41:07,180 --> 00:41:07,520
Meaning.

937
00:41:07,940 --> 00:41:12,970
This means to make sure everyone agrees on what is most important before continuing work.

938
00:41:13,430 --> 00:41:13,790
Usage.

939
00:41:14,070 --> 00:41:18,390
You use it in meetings when multiple tasks or goals need coordination.

940
00:41:18,830 --> 00:41:20,030
Example conversation.

941
00:41:20,120 --> 00:41:24,660
We have marketing updates, sales targets, and product issues to discuss.

942
00:41:25,100 --> 00:41:28,910
Before we proceed, let's align our priorities so we don't lose focus.

943
00:41:29,410 --> 00:41:32,130
Next sentence, I see where you're coming from.

944
00:41:32,150 --> 00:41:36,410
Meaning, you understand someone's opinion even if you don't fully agree.

945
00:41:36,450 --> 00:41:41,920
Usage, you use it to show respect during discussions or disagreements.

946
00:41:41,940 --> 00:41:42,960
Example conversation.

947
00:41:43,400 --> 00:41:46,560
We should delay the launch to fix all bugs first.

948
00:41:46,730 --> 00:41:50,350
I see where you're coming from, but we also have strict deadlines to meet.

949
00:41:50,690 --> 00:41:54,040
Next sentence, let's break this down step by step.

950
00:41:54,520 --> 00:41:57,860
Meaning, to analyze something in smaller, easier parts.

951
00:41:58,480 --> 00:42:02,670
Usage, you use it when explaining complex problems or plans.

952
00:42:03,110 --> 00:42:04,130
Example conversation.

953
00:42:04,570 --> 00:42:07,990
The client wants a full system redesign in two months.

954
00:42:08,490 --> 00:42:09,860
That sounds complex.

955
00:42:09,880 --> 00:42:11,740
Let's break this down step by step.

956
00:42:12,220 --> 00:42:16,040
Next sentence, we need to be realistic about timelines.

957
00:42:16,210 --> 00:42:19,890
Meaning, to consider practical and achievable deadlines.

958
00:42:19,910 --> 00:42:20,270
Usage.

959
00:42:20,750 --> 00:42:24,980
You use it when planning projects to avoid unrealistic expectations.

960
00:42:25,500 --> 00:42:26,500
Example conversation.

961
00:42:26,820 --> 00:42:29,000
Can we finish everything in one week?

962
00:42:29,440 --> 00:42:31,710
We need to be realistic about timelines.

963
00:42:32,030 --> 00:42:33,490
It will take at least three weeks.

964
00:42:33,970 --> 00:42:34,730
Next sentence.

965
00:42:35,290 --> 00:42:36,950
Let's not jump to conclusions.

966
00:42:37,530 --> 00:42:40,920
Meaning, not to make decisions or assumptions too quickly.

967
00:42:41,070 --> 00:42:44,370
Usage, you use it when more information is needed.

968
00:42:44,850 --> 00:42:45,850
Example conversation.

969
00:42:46,310 --> 00:42:47,610
Sales dropped this week.

970
00:42:47,950 --> 00:42:49,380
The new strategy is failing.

971
00:42:49,840 --> 00:42:51,440
Let's not jump to conclusions.

972
00:42:51,640 --> 00:42:53,060
We need full data first.

973
00:42:53,540 --> 00:42:56,220
Next sentence, can we revisit this later?

974
00:42:56,730 --> 00:42:59,830
Meaning, to discuss something again at a different time.

975
00:43:00,330 --> 00:43:04,200
Usage, you use it when a topic is important but not urgent.

976
00:43:04,660 --> 00:43:05,640
Example conversation.

977
00:43:06,100 --> 00:43:08,540
We still need to decide on the pricing model.

978
00:43:08,740 --> 00:43:11,980
Can we revisit this later after reviewing competitor data?

979
00:43:12,340 --> 00:43:15,970
Next sentence, that's outside the scope of this discussion.

980
00:43:16,430 --> 00:43:19,450
Meaning, the topic is not related to the current meeting.

981
00:43:19,910 --> 00:43:22,790
Usage, you use it to stay focused in meetings.

982
00:43:23,260 --> 00:43:24,260
Example conversation.

983
00:43:24,660 --> 00:43:27,100
Should we also redesign the company logo?

984
00:43:27,560 --> 00:43:29,720
That's outside the scope of this discussion.

985
00:43:29,920 --> 00:43:31,580
Let's focus on the project plan.

986
00:43:32,060 --> 00:43:35,730
Next sentence, we're under pressure to deliver results.

987
00:43:35,890 --> 00:43:39,660
Meaning, there is strong expectation to achieve outcomes quickly.

988
00:43:40,240 --> 00:43:43,990
Usage, you use it in high-pressure work environments.

989
00:43:44,010 --> 00:43:44,990
Example conversation.

990
00:43:45,450 --> 00:43:47,430
Why is the team working late this week?

991
00:43:47,890 --> 00:43:51,180
We're under pressure to deliver results before the client deadline.

992
00:43:51,620 --> 00:43:55,220
Next sentence, let's keep communication transparent.

993
00:43:55,700 --> 00:43:58,610
Meaning, to share information openly and honestly.

994
00:43:59,090 --> 00:44:02,650
Usage, you use it in teamwork and leadership situations.

995
00:44:03,130 --> 00:44:04,300
Example conversation.

996
00:44:04,390 --> 00:44:06,820
Should we inform the client about the delay?

997
00:44:06,880 --> 00:44:10,520
Yes, let's keep communication transparent from the beginning.

998
00:44:10,940 --> 00:44:14,640
Next sentence, I'll take ownership of this task.

999
00:44:14,660 --> 00:44:17,290
Meaning, you are responsible for completing a task.

1000
00:44:17,810 --> 00:44:20,990
Usage, you use it to show accountability in work.

1001
00:44:21,470 --> 00:44:22,470
Example conversation.

1002
00:44:22,890 --> 00:44:24,720
Who will handle the client follow-up?

1003
00:44:25,200 --> 00:44:28,220
I'll take ownership of this task and update the team daily.

1004
00:44:28,700 --> 00:44:32,320
Next sentence, let's evaluate the risks first.

1005
00:44:32,470 --> 00:44:36,060
Meaning, to analyze possible problems before making a decision.

1006
00:44:36,540 --> 00:44:40,060
Usage, you use it in planning and strategy discussions.

1007
00:44:40,540 --> 00:44:41,550
Example conversation.

1008
00:44:41,930 --> 00:44:43,670
Should we launch the product early?

1009
00:44:43,710 --> 00:44:46,790
Let's evaluate the risks first before deciding.

1010
00:44:47,270 --> 00:44:50,640
Next sentence, we need to streamline this process.

1011
00:44:51,160 --> 00:44:53,940
Meaning, to make a process simpler and more efficient.

1012
00:44:54,400 --> 00:44:58,010
Usage, you use it when improving workflow or systems.

1013
00:44:58,510 --> 00:44:59,630
Example conversation.

1014
00:44:59,780 --> 00:45:02,300
Too many steps are slowing down approvals.

1015
00:45:02,820 --> 00:45:05,780
Yes, we need to streamline this process to save time.

1016
00:45:06,240 --> 00:45:09,410
Next sentence, let's not overlook the details.

1017
00:45:09,930 --> 00:45:12,850
Meaning, not to ignore small but important points.

1018
00:45:13,470 --> 00:45:16,490
Usage, you use it when accuracy is important.

1019
00:45:16,950 --> 00:45:17,960
Example conversation.

1020
00:45:18,360 --> 00:45:19,920
We finished the report quickly.

1021
00:45:20,320 --> 00:45:23,560
Good, but let's not overlook the details before submitting it.

1022
00:45:24,040 --> 00:45:27,410
Next sentence, we're on track to meet the deadline.

1023
00:45:27,520 --> 00:45:29,680
Meaning, progress is going as planned.

1024
00:45:30,180 --> 00:45:33,090
Usage, you use it to report positive progress.

1025
00:45:33,570 --> 00:45:34,550
Example conversation.

1026
00:45:34,970 --> 00:45:36,130
How is the project going?

1027
00:45:36,530 --> 00:45:38,910
We're on track to meet the deadline without delays.

1028
00:45:39,410 --> 00:45:42,300
Next sentence, let's escalate this issue.

1029
00:45:42,780 --> 00:45:45,220
Meaning, to bring a problem to higher management.

1030
00:45:45,780 --> 00:45:49,650
Usage, you use it when an issue cannot be solved at your level.

1031
00:45:50,070 --> 00:45:51,050
Example conversation.

1032
00:45:51,510 --> 00:45:53,630
The client is unhappy with the delay.

1033
00:45:54,050 --> 00:45:57,480
We should escalate this issue to the project manager immediately.

1034
00:45:57,660 --> 00:46:01,160
Next sentence, I'd like to propose an alternative.

1035
00:46:01,660 --> 00:46:04,450
Meaning, suggesting a different idea or solution.

1036
00:46:04,970 --> 00:46:08,660
Usage, you use it in meetings when offering new options.

1037
00:46:09,200 --> 00:46:10,220
Example conversation.

1038
00:46:10,620 --> 00:46:12,740
We should increase the budget for marketing.

1039
00:46:13,200 --> 00:46:17,230
I'd like to propose an alternative that focuses on digital ads instead.

1040
00:46:17,690 --> 00:46:21,040
Next sentence, let's stay focused on the objective.

1041
00:46:21,480 --> 00:46:24,020
Meaning, to keep attention on the main goal.

1042
00:46:24,150 --> 00:46:27,820
Usage, you use it when discussions become off-topic.

1043
00:46:28,240 --> 00:46:29,240
Example conversation.

1044
00:46:29,660 --> 00:46:32,390
We should also redesign the website completely.

1045
00:46:32,890 --> 00:46:36,810
Let's stay focused on the objective, which is improving user engagement.

1046
00:46:36,830 --> 00:46:40,200
Next sentence, we need to manage expectations.

1047
00:46:40,700 --> 00:46:43,860
Meaning, making sure people understand what is realistic.

1048
00:46:44,420 --> 00:46:48,050
Usage, you use it in client communication or leadership.

1049
00:46:48,450 --> 00:46:49,470
Example conversation.

1050
00:46:49,830 --> 00:46:52,240
Can we deliver everything in two days?

1051
00:46:52,390 --> 00:46:56,210
We need to manage expectations and explain the real timeline.

1052
00:46:56,550 --> 00:46:59,790
Next sentence, let's take a data-driven approach.

1053
00:47:00,270 --> 00:47:03,470
Meaning, making decisions based on facts and analysis.

1054
00:47:04,020 --> 00:47:06,780
Usage, you use it in strategic planning.

1055
00:47:07,180 --> 00:47:08,180
Example conversation.

1056
00:47:08,600 --> 00:47:11,320
How should we choose between these two strategies?

1057
00:47:11,780 --> 00:47:15,730
Let's take a data-driven approach and analyze performance metrics first.

1058
00:47:16,210 --> 00:47:19,810
Next sentence, we should document everything clearly.

1059
00:47:19,960 --> 00:47:24,020
Meaning, to record all information properly for future use.

1060
00:47:24,040 --> 00:47:27,230
Usage, you use it in professional project management.

1061
00:47:27,630 --> 00:47:28,650
Example conversation.

1062
00:47:29,130 --> 00:47:31,510
What if someone forgets the meeting decisions?

1063
00:47:32,010 --> 00:47:35,380
That's why we should document everything clearly after each meeting.

1064
00:47:35,820 --> 00:47:39,190
Final sentence, let's summarize the key takeaways.

1065
00:47:39,730 --> 00:47:42,610
Meaning, to highlight the most important points at the end.

1066
00:47:43,170 --> 00:47:46,880
Usage, you use it when closing meetings or presentations.

1067
00:47:47,080 --> 00:47:47,400
Great.

1068
00:47:47,620 --> 00:47:50,340
Let's summarize the key takeaways before we end the meeting.

1069
00:47:50,760 --> 00:47:55,870
That brings us to the end of today's Business English Sentences for Fluent Speaking session.

1070
00:47:56,250 --> 00:48:01,640
We hope this real office-style conversation helped you understand how to use professional English naturally.

1071
00:48:01,960 --> 00:48:07,030
Practice these sentences in real situations or repeat them aloud to build fluency.

1072
00:48:07,450 --> 00:48:10,910
The more you use them, the more confident you'll become in business communication.

1073
00:48:11,250 --> 00:48:12,410
Thanks for listening, everyone.

1074
00:48:12,830 --> 00:48:15,120
And we'll see you in the next Business English episode.

1075
00:48:15,440 --> 00:48:16,780
Take care and goodbye.

